3.8 | Implementation of Tree-Level Aggregation
The second federated configuration in this study will implement a tree-level aggregation
strategy, wherein each federated client will train multiple Isolation Forest models
locally and will transmit only sanitized decision tree structures to a central server.
Unlike score-level aggregation, anomaly scores will remain entirely local to each
client and will be used solely for threshold calibration and performance evaluation.
Global aggregation will be performed exclusively at the model-structure level, where
decision trees learned across distributed clients will be pooled to construct a unified
global anomaly detector. This design will enable structural knowledge sharing across
decentralized data partitions while preserving data privacy, as no raw records, feature
matrices, labels, or anomaly scores will be exchanged during the federated process.

