Chapter 3. Research Methodology 3.8. Implementation of Tree-Level Aggregation

The tree-level aggregation strategy will operate across three federated rounds (R
= 3). In each round, every client will execute the complete local anomaly detection
pipeline independently on its server-partitioned dataset subset. At the beginning
of each round, clients will initialize their local execution context by setting round
identifiers, configuring random seeds for reproducibility, and optionally loading global
feedback (e.g., the aggregated global anomaly threshold) from the previous round
when available. Each client will perform local preprocessing on its assigned dataset
partition. To ensure a consistent feature space across all clients, preprocessing will
incorporate a shared global StandardScaler. The scaler parameters will be computed
centrally during dataset initialization prior to data partitioning and will be distributed
to all clients as fixed preprocessing parameters. These parameters will remain constant
across federated rounds and will not require access to client-side raw data, thereby
maintaining feature alignment while preserving data privacy. All local Isolation Forest
models will be initialized using a uniform hyperparameter configuration derived from
centralized tuning, including 100 estimators, 0.1 maximum samples, 0.5 maximum
features, and a contamination rate of 0.02. Proportional adjustments will be applied
to chunk-based models to ensure memory efficiency without compromising fairness or
comparability across clients.
Local training will follow a dual-level parallel execution framework. First,
intra-model parallelism (chunk-level) will be applied by partitioning the local training
data into multiple chunks, with each chunk used to train an individual Isolation Forest
model. Second, inter-model parallelism (ensemble-level) will be employed by training
multiple full-data Isolation Forest models in parallel on the entire local training set, as
described in Section 3.4.
After local training, each client will perform local validation and threshold selection.
Validation anomaly scores will be computed from all locally trained models and
aggregated only within the client to form a unified validation score vector. An optimal
decision threshold will then be selected by maximizing the F1-score on the validation
set. These anomaly scores and thresholds will not be transmitted to the server and will
be used exclusively for local calibration and performance assessment. Subsequently,
local testing will be conducted using the calibrated threshold to compute precision,
recall, and F1-score metrics. Execution time and dataset statistics will also be recorded
to support system-level evaluation.
Prior to transmission, all locally trained models will undergo privacy-oriented
sanitization performed entirely at the client side. This process will remove impurity

