contributions from all clients while mitigating the influence of outliers. The resulting
global forest and aggregation statistics will be stored for evaluation and optional
feedback to clients in subsequent rounds. In later rounds, clients may incorporate global
feedback, such as the aggregated global threshold, while retraining local models on their
assigned data partitions. This iterative design will enable progressive refinement of
the global anomaly detection model while maintaining strict data locality and privacy
guarantees.

3.9.2 | Tree-Level Aggregation Evaluation
The tree-level aggregation strategy will be evaluated with a focus on global
model quality, communication overhead associated with transmitting tree structures,
aggregation cost at the server, and the diversity of the merged forest.
A. Global Model Quality Across Clients – The global federated forest will be
evaluated on each client’s test set to assess its robustness across non-IID data
distributions. Per-client precision, recall, and F1-scores will indicate whether
structural tree aggregation produces a more stable and generalizable anomaly
detector.
B. Communication Cost of Model Transmission – This metric measures the size (in
KB/MB) of the sanitized tree packages transmitted by each client. As entire Isolation
Forest structures are exchanged, this evaluation reflects the true communication
overhead incurred by tree-level federated learning in each round.

