C. Aggregation Overhead per Round – This criterion quantifies server-side
computational cost, including tree deserialization, pooling, sampling, and
reconstruction of the global forest. Aggregation time reflects the computational
burden required to merge large numbers of trees across multiple clients.
D. Tree Diversity and Structural Scale – This measure reports the total number of trees
contributed by all clients and the number retained after enforcing the global tree cap.
Diversity analysis highlights how chunk-level parallelism, ensemble construction,
and heterogeneous datasets contribute to enriching the final global forest structure.

